---
import MainLayout from "../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout
    title="Letters From the River | The River Kept Flowing"
    description="Letters From the River is a growing archive of personal letters about fatherhood, marriage, faith, forgiveness, memory, responsibility, ordinary life, and the lessons carried downstream."
>
<main data-pagefind-body>


        <header class="letters-hero">

            <div class="letters-hero-content">

                <p class="eyebrow">
                    Words preserved for those downstream
                </p>

                <h1>
                    Very few people leave their heart behind.
                </h1>

                <p class="letters-hero-lead">
                    These letters preserve what I have loved, feared, learned,
                    questioned, forgiven, and hoped someone might still understand
                    long after the original moment has passed.
                </p>

                <div class="letters-hero-actions">

                    <a href="#letter-archive" class="button letters-button-primary">
                        Open the Letters
                    </a>

                    <a href="#why-i-write" class="button letters-button-secondary">
                        Why I Write Them
                    </a>

                </div>

            </div>

        </header>


        <section class="letters-opening" id="why-i-write">

            <div class="reading-shell">

                <p class="section-label">
                    Why these letters exist
                </p>

                <h2>
                    Every person leaves something behind.
                </h2>

                <div class="letters-opening-lines">

                    <p>
                        Some leave money.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Some leave property.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Some leave businesses.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Some leave photographs, possessions, names, and unfinished plans.
                    </p>

                </div>

                <p class="letters-opening-lead">
                    I want to leave enough of my inner life that the people I love
                    can still know me when memory is no longer enough.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I want my sons to know what I believed before they were old
                    enough to ask.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I want my wife to have a record of what I saw in her during
                    seasons when life moved too quickly for me to say it well.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I want to remember the people who formed me, the mistakes that
                    corrected me, the grief that softened me, and the ordinary days
                    that seemed small while I was living them.
                </p>

                <p>
                    These letters are not written from the safety of a finished life.
                </p>

                <p>
                    They are written from inside the river.
                </p>

            </div>

        </section>


        <section class="letters-featured">

            <div class="section-shell">

                <div class="letters-featured-grid">

                    <div class="letters-featured-heading">

                        <p class="section-label light-label">
                            The first letter
                        </p>

                        <h2>
                            To my sons, before you understand why I wrote this.
                        </h2>

                        <p>
                            A letter does not need to wait until the lesson is complete.
                            Sometimes it must be written while love is still learning
                            how to carry the responsibility it has been given.
                        </p>

                    </div>


                    <article class="featured-letter-card">

                        <div class="featured-letter-meta">

                            <p>
                                Letter No. 001
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                To My Sons
                            </p>

                        </div>

                        <div class="featured-letter-body">

                            <p>
                                My sons,
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                You may one day read these words when you are old
                                enough to see me as more than your father.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                You may see the parts of me that were uncertain,
                                impatient, tired, ambitious, afraid, and still becoming.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                I hope that does not weaken your understanding of me.
                                I hope it makes my love more believable.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                I did not wait until I became perfect to love you.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                I loved you while I was still learning how to become
                                the kind of man capable of carrying that love well.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                Much of what I built was built because I wanted to
                                be present when your lives were happening.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                I wanted you to remember more than a provider who
                                was always working somewhere else.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                I wanted you to remember walks, meals, questions,
                                laughter, correction, prayer, stories, and the feeling
                                that your life was never an interruption to mine.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                You were part of the reason I wanted my life to become
                                something worth remembering.
                            </p>

                            <p class="featured-letter-closing">
                                Love,<br>
                                Dad
                            </p>

                        </div>

                    </article>

                </div>

            </div>

        </section>


        <section class="letters-archive" id="letter-archive">

            <div class="section-shell">

                <div class="letters-section-heading">

                    <p class="section-label">
                        The letter archive
                    </p>

                    <h2>
                        Letters written to people, seasons, memories, and questions.
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        Each collection will grow as the life behind it continues
                        unfolding.
                    </p>

                </div>


                <div class="letters-collection-grid">


                    <article class="letter-collection-card letter-collection-featured">

                        <span class="letter-collection-number">
                            01
                        </span>

                        <p class="letter-collection-type">
                            For those who come after me
                        </p>

                        <h3>
                            Letters to My Sons
                        </h3>

                        <p>
                            Love, instruction, apology, memory, prayer, protection,
                            fatherhood, family history, and everything I hope my sons
                            understand when they eventually begin building lives of
                            their own.
                        </p>

                        <div class="letter-collection-topics">

                            <span>Fatherhood</span>
                            <span>Legacy</span>
                            <span>Character</span>
                            <span>Family</span>

                        </div>

                        <a href="#" class="letter-link light-letter-link">
                            Letters coming soon
                            <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                        </a>

                    </article>


                    <article class="letter-collection-card">

                        <span class="letter-collection-number">
                            02
                        </span>

                        <p class="letter-collection-type">
                            For the woman beside me
                        </p>

                        <h3>
                            Letters to My Wife
                        </h3>

                        <p>
                            Gratitude, admiration, memory, promises, apologies,
                            difficult seasons, quiet sacrifices, shared dreams, and
                            the things I never want familiarity to make me stop noticing.
                        </p>

                        <div class="letter-collection-topics">

                            <span>Marriage</span>
                            <span>Love</span>
                            <span>Gratitude</span>
                            <span>Home</span>

                        </div>

                        <a href="#" class="letter-link">
                            Letters coming soon
                            <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                        </a>

                    </article>


                    <article class="letter-collection-card">

                        <span class="letter-collection-number">
                            03
                        </span>

                        <p class="letter-collection-type">
                            Perspective carried backward
                        </p>

                        <h3>
                            Letters to My Younger Self
                        </h3>

                        <p>
                            The truths I could not yet understand, the ambitions
                            that would change shape, the warnings I may not have
                            listened to, and the compassion I now have for the person
                            still trying to survive what he could not explain.
                        </p>

                        <div class="letter-collection-topics">

                            <span>Identity</span>
                            <span>Growth</span>
                            <span>Regret</span>
                            <span>Perspective</span>

                        </div>

                        <a href="#" class="letter-link">
                            Letters coming soon
                            <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                        </a>

                    </article>


                    <article class="letter-collection-card">

                        <span class="letter-collection-number">
                            04
                        </span>

                        <p class="letter-collection-type">
                            For those beneath the weight
                        </p>

                        <h3>
                            Letters to the Man Carrying Too Much
                        </h3>

                        <p>
                            Words for husbands, fathers, providers, builders,
                            salespeople, leaders, and men who feel responsible for
                            everyone while quietly wondering how much longer they
                            can continue carrying themselves.
                        </p>

                        <div class="letter-collection-topics">

                            <span>Responsibility</span>
                            <span>Work</span>
                            <span>Peace</span>
                            <span>Endurance</span>

                        </div>

                        <a href="#" class="letter-link">
                            Letters coming soon
                            <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                        </a>

                    </article>


                    <article class="letter-collection-card">

                        <span class="letter-collection-number">
                            05
                        </span>

                        <p class="letter-collection-type">
                            Written from the walk itself
                        </p>

                        <h3>
                            Letters About God
                        </h3>

                        <p>
                            Trust, silence, obedience, waiting, temptation, mercy,
                            conviction, surrender, Scripture, disappointment, prayer,
                            and what it means to follow God without always understanding
                            where the path is leading.
                        </p>

                        <div class="letter-collection-topics">

                            <span>Faith</span>
                            <span>Waiting</span>
                            <span>Obedience</span>
                            <span>Grace</span>

                        </div>

                        <a href="#" class="letter-link">
                            Letters coming soon
                            <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                        </a>

                    </article>


                    <article class="letter-collection-card">

                        <span class="letter-collection-number">
                            06
                        </span>

                        <p class="letter-collection-type">
                            Words without an address
                        </p>

                        <h3>
                            Letters I May Never Send
                        </h3>

                        <p>
                            Letters shaped by forgiveness, grief, distance, betrayal,
                            admiration, unfinished conversations, people who changed
                            me, and the words that still deserve to exist even when
                            they never reach their original recipient.
                        </p>

                        <div class="letter-collection-topics">

                            <span>Forgiveness</span>
                            <span>Grief</span>
                            <span>Memory</span>
                            <span>Healing</span>

                        </div>

                        <a href="#" class="letter-link">
                            Letters coming soon
                            <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                        </a>

                    </article>


                    <article class="letter-collection-card letter-collection-wide">

                        <span class="letter-collection-number">
                            07
                        </span>

                        <p class="letter-collection-type">
                            The life hidden inside normal days
                        </p>

                        <h3>
                            Letters From Ordinary Days
                        </h3>

                        <p>
                            Walks with my son, meals cooked for my family, insurance
                            calls, unfinished websites, quiet evenings, desert skies,
                            household routines, laughter, exhaustion, and the small
                            details that may one day become the memories I would give
                            anything to enter again.
                        </p>

                        <div class="letter-collection-topics">

                            <span>Daily Life</span>
                            <span>Memory</span>
                            <span>Family</span>
                            <span>Gratitude</span>

                        </div>

                        <a href="#" class="letter-link">
                            Letters coming soon
                            <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                        </a>

                    </article>


                </div>

            </div>

        </section>


        <section class="letters-ordinary">

            <div class="reading-shell">

                <p class="section-label light-label">
                    The ordinary deserves remembering
                </p>

                <h2>
                    Most of a life does not announce that it is becoming a memory.
                </h2>

                <p class="letters-ordinary-lead">
                    It simply happens while we are busy waiting for something larger.
                </p>

                <p>
                    A child asks to go outside.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Dinner is cooking.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The same show plays in the background while work continues
                    between phone calls.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Someone laughs from another room.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The house is imperfect. The future is uncertain. The bills still
                    exist. The next goal remains unfinished.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Yet years later, those may be the scenes we most desperately
                    wish we could enter again.
                </p>

                <blockquote class="letters-ordinary-quote">
                    The ordinary day rarely knows that it is sacred while it is
                    still being lived.
                </blockquote>

                <p>
                    These letters are one way of noticing before the moment disappears.
                </p>

            </div>

        </section>


        <section class="letters-purpose">

            <div class="section-shell letters-purpose-grid">

                <div class="letters-purpose-heading">

                    <p class="section-label">
                        What a letter can carry
                    </p>

                    <h2>
                        A letter preserves more than information.
                    </h2>

                </div>


                <div class="letters-purpose-list">


                    <article class="letters-purpose-item">

                        <span>
                            01
                        </span>

                        <div>

                            <h3>
                                A letter can preserve a voice.
                            </h3>

                            <p>
                                It captures the way a person thought, noticed,
                                hesitated, hoped, and spoke before time softened
                                everything into a simpler story.
                            </p>

                        </div>

                    </article>


                    <article class="letters-purpose-item">

                        <span>
                            02
                        </span>

                        <div>

                            <h3>
                                A letter can tell the truth without demanding an answer.
                            </h3>

                            <p>
                                Some words need to be expressed even when no reply
                                is expected, possible, or wise.
                            </p>

                        </div>

                    </article>


                    <article class="letters-purpose-item">

                        <span>
                            03
                        </span>

                        <div>

                            <h3>
                                A letter can reach the right season later.
                            </h3>

                            <p>
                                Words that mean little to a child may become
                                inheritance when read by the adult that child becomes.
                            </p>

                        </div>

                    </article>


                    <article class="letters-purpose-item">

                        <span>
                            04
                        </span>

                        <div>

                            <h3>
                                A letter can make love visible.
                            </h3>

                            <p>
                                People should not have to guess what they meant to
                                us after we are no longer able to tell them.
                            </p>

                        </div>

                    </article>


                    <article class="letters-purpose-item">

                        <span>
                            05
                        </span>

                        <div>

                            <h3>
                                A letter can interrupt inherited silence.
                            </h3>

                            <p>
                                Families often pass down what was never discussed.
                                Writing can name what previous generations carried
                                without language.
                            </p>

                        </div>

                    </article>


                    <article class="letters-purpose-item">

                        <span>
                            06
                        </span>

                        <div>

                            <h3>
                                A letter can keep wisdom from disappearing.
                            </h3>

                            <p>
                                A hard-earned lesson becomes inheritance only when
                                someone takes responsibility for preserving it.
                            </p>

                        </div>

                    </article>


                </div>

            </div>

        </section>


        <section class="letters-unwritten">

            <div class="section-shell">

                <div class="letters-section-heading letters-unwritten-heading">

                    <p class="section-label light-label">
                        Letters still unwritten
                    </p>

                    <h2>
                        Some letters are already waiting inside the life ahead.
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        These are a few of the letters I know I will eventually need
                        to write.
                    </p>

                </div>


                <div class="letters-unwritten-grid">


                    <article class="unwritten-letter-card">

                        <p class="unwritten-letter-recipient">
                            To my firstborn
                        </p>

                        <h3>
                            The day you made me a father
                        </h3>

                        <p>
                            About the person I was before you arrived and the
                            responsibility your life awakened in me.
                        </p>

                    </article>


                    <article class="unwritten-letter-card">

                        <p class="unwritten-letter-recipient">
                            To my second son
                        </p>

                        <h3>
                            Before I knew your face
                        </h3>

                        <p>
                            About waiting for you, preparing for you, and how your
                            existence changed our family before you entered the room.
                        </p>

                    </article>


                    <article class="unwritten-letter-card">

                        <p class="unwritten-letter-recipient">
                            To my wife
                        </p>

                        <h3>
                            What I saw you carry
                        </h3>

                        <p>
                            A record of the strength, discomfort, sacrifice, and
                            love involved in carrying our children.
                        </p>

                    </article>


                    <article class="unwritten-letter-card">

                        <p class="unwritten-letter-recipient">
                            To the man I used to be
                        </p>

                        <h3>
                            You were not weak for being tired
                        </h3>

                        <p>
                            About survival, ambition, burnout, responsibility, and
                            what I misunderstood about rest.
                        </p>

                    </article>


                    <article class="unwritten-letter-card">

                        <p class="unwritten-letter-recipient">
                            To the father working too much
                        </p>

                        <h3>
                            Your children are not waiting for your success
                        </h3>

                        <p>
                            About provision, presence, regret, enough, and the cost
                            of postponing a relationship until life becomes easier.
                        </p>

                    </article>


                    <article class="unwritten-letter-card">

                        <p class="unwritten-letter-recipient">
                            To whoever finds this later
                        </p>

                        <h3>
                            I hope you know I was still becoming
                        </h3>

                        <p>
                            A final reminder that these words were written by a
                            human being inside an unfinished life.
                        </p>

                    </article>


                </div>

            </div>

        </section>


        <section class="letters-reader">

            <div class="reading-shell">

                <p class="section-label">
                    To the person reading
                </p>

                <h2>
                    Some letters reach people they were never originally written for.
                </h2>

                <p>
                    You may not be my son.
                </p>

                <p>
                    You may not know my family.
                </p>

                <p>
                    You may have arrived here while carrying a life very different
                    from mine.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Yet grief recognizes grief. Responsibility recognizes
                    responsibility. Love recognizes the fear of losing what it has
                    been given.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Perhaps something written to my children will help you understand
                    your father.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Perhaps something written to my younger self will give you more
                    patience with the person you used to be.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Perhaps something written from an ordinary day will remind you
                    to notice your own before it becomes a memory.
                </p>

                <blockquote class="letters-reader-quote">
                    A letter can belong to its recipient and still carry something
                    useful to everyone who recognizes themselves inside it.
                </blockquote>

            </div>

        </section>


        <section class="letters-navigation">

            <div class="section-shell">

                <div class="letters-section-heading">

                    <p class="section-label light-label">
                        Continue downstream
                    </p>

                    <h2>
                        Read the life around the letters.
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        See the story that formed them, the present season producing
                        them, and the practical lessons being gathered from them.
                    </p>

                </div>


                <div class="letters-navigation-grid">


                    <article class="letters-navigation-card letters-navigation-featured">

                        <p class="letters-navigation-type">
                            The history
                        </p>

                        <h3>
                            The Source
                        </h3>

                        <p>
                            Read the memories, losses, ambitions, experiences, and
                            turning points behind the voice writing these letters.
                        </p>

                        <a href="/source/" class="letter-link light-letter-link">
                            Read the Story
                            <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                        </a>

                    </article>


                    <article class="letters-navigation-card">

                        <p class="letters-navigation-type">
                            The present
                        </p>

                        <h3>
                            The Current
                        </h3>

                        <p>
                            See what I am building, learning, questioning, and
                            carrying in this season of life.
                        </p>

                        <a href="/current/" class="letter-link">
                            Enter the Current
                            <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                        </a>

                    </article>


                    <article class="letters-navigation-card">

                        <p class="letters-navigation-type">
                            The direction
                        </p>

                        <h3>
                            The Journey
                        </h3>

                        <p>
                            Follow the commitments, goals, milestones, uncertainty,
                            and direction taking shape downstream.
                        </p>

                        <a href="/journey/" class="letter-link">
                            Continue the Journey
                            <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                        </a>

                    </article>


                    <article class="letters-navigation-card">

                        <p class="letters-navigation-type">
                            The practical lessons
                        </p>

                        <h3>
                            River Guides
                        </h3>

                        <p>
                            Move from personal reflection into structured guidance,
                            resources, explanations, and tools.
                        </p>

                        <a href="/river-guides/" class="letter-link">
                            Explore River Guides
                            <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                        </a>

                    </article>


                </div>

            </div>

        </section>


        <section class="letters-final">

            <div class="reading-shell">

                <p class="section-label">
                    Until the next letter
                </p>

                <h2>
                    These words do not need to reach everyone.
                </h2>

                <p>
                    They only need to reach the person who recognizes something
                    true inside them.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The person who needed to know they were not alone.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The child who wanted to understand a parent.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The father who needed permission to come home emotionally.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The person trying to forgive without pretending nothing happened.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The person who nearly missed the beauty of an ordinary day.
                </p>

                <blockquote class="letters-final-quote">
                    If these letters help someone feel remembered, understood,
                    forgiven, warned, loved, or less alone, then they have already
                    done what I hoped they would do.
                </blockquote>

                <p class="letters-signature">
                    Until the next bend,<br>
                    River
                </p>

                <div class="page-actions">

                    <a href="/current/" class="button page-button-primary">
                        Enter the Current
                    </a>

                    <a href="/river-guides/" class="button page-button-secondary">
                        Explore River Guides
                    </a>

                </div>

            </div>

        </section>


    </main>

</MainLayout>
